d  simply closing down more roots making  the environment more hostile is not the  answer the answer is for there to be  safe ways for people who are in most  cases those who are  coming to the country through these  informal methods when they actually put  their case uh to the test they have  proven to be genuine refugees to have a  real right to the protection that the  1951 convention affords them so what  we're asking for is proper safe routes  to come into the uk not a continual  ratcheting up of more and more hostility  but but what of the government's point  that that if these flights take off even  if there are a small number of people on  board them  it will send a message to both those who  who would seek to get on the boats and  the people smugglers making money from  that misery to discourage them from  doing it in this dangerous and illegal  way and would encourage people to do it  at a legal route  i mean even the i think the senior  permanent secretary is in the home  office said there's no evidence that  that will be the case and immediately  after the policy was announced i think  for a few days there was a  suggestion there were fewer people  crossing the channel then the weather  cleared up  and suddenly the numbers were back to  what they've been before there's  absolutely no evidence to date that this  is going to act as a major deterrent on  people and  putting people's uh  rights at risk in the way that this is  happening rejecting people who have a  legitimate uh claim to asylum to refugee  status we just don't think is there is a  right way to go about it  do you think we as a nation have become  perhaps a little bit less tolerant or  less  compassionate over the last few years i  don't know if it's something linked to  covert or the fact that we are all very  much isolated within very small groups  but do you think generally there is less  tolerance in community for for others in  inverted commerce  um no i wouldn't i wouldn't say that  entirely look at the way that people  have responded to the ukrainian crisis  there's been an overwhelming uh welcome  for people who've been making their way  here from there in far surpassing i  think the other government's own uh  expectations in that but i think i think  at times people fear they can be they  can be led into fearing that somehow  these are  these refugees are going to change our  society and we're actually talking about  pretty small numbers who who end up in  the small boats and the numbers who come  when britain is not the main destination  for refugees in europe there are far  more refugees in other parts of the of  the european area and so you know we  have to pay  play our part take take our share of  those who are really fleeing war and  terror and persecution in their  homelands and i think once it's  presented  in that way i think people will will  support as they support the ukrainians  you talk about people perhaps some  people being scared fear and and you  raised of course that the homes for  ukraine scheme that the government um  the government came up with  what do you think about that when you  look at that and you contrast the way  that those are being welcomed from  ukraine you mentioned fear  do you think racism plays a part that  that people are more welcoming of those  in this country who look more like them  i think we all have an innate tendency  to to to feel more naturally  a kinship with people who look a bit  like us and we and seem to come from a  similar background but actually what  enriches our society is it is very  diversity i'm fortunate enough to live  in extremely diverse city in manchester  where generations of people have come  here often as refugees bringing their  talents and their skills adding to the  economy and the value of the community  and and so i think once people get to  know as real human beings those who are  coming and fleeing war and famine and  terror in other places i think those  attitudes do 